In article <1993May7.201619.2698@ugle.unit.no> hafting@Lise.Unit.NO (Helge Hafting) writes:
% 
% I am trying to change the game a bit in order to allow for different types
% of coins.

%   There are two ways to implement weight difference, I would like to
% know what people prefer:
% 
% 1.  One coin is always worth one zorkmid, regardless of metal.  Copper coins
%     will have to be heavy...

% 2.  The method I prefer, as it seems more realistic:
%     Copper is used for small change, so copper coins have a low value.
%     The coins will still have different weights, but copper coins may now
%     be lighter than gold pieces.  (But the same amount of zorkmids will of
%     course still be heavier in copper than in gold.)

Option #2 definitely.  Can you imagine the confusion inherent in #1?
Everyone "knows" that copper is worth less than gold, and that gold is
heavier.


% There are two ways to implement different values, if weight method 2 is used:
% 
% a. A gold piece is worth 1 zorkmid, so gold keeps its traditional value.
%    Coins made of lesser metal will have to have fractional values.

% b. Normal coins don't have fractional values, a copper piece may
%    have value 1, and a gold piece perhaps 20.

"a" makes more sense -- prices seem to be more in line with gold than
copper -- but then (IMHO) no one would bother to deal with copper
coins.  Maybe if you made characters a little stronger to handle the
extra weight...


% It seems to me that mithril is some sort of metal.  (I might be
% wrong, I haven't heard of this stuff anywhere but in nethack.)
% Should I implement mithril coins, or is that stupid?

Mithril is of _Lord_of_the_Rings_ fame.  I think it's a kind of
silver.  (Tolkien seems to use "mithril" and "mithril silver"
interchangeably.)  You might want to check this with
rec.arts.books.tolkien or alt.fan.tolkien to verify.

In any case, mithril is valuable stuff, and probably doesn't make much
sense as a coin.  


% When it comes to metal prices, should I use today's metal prices
% (what are they, anyway?) or should I use "ancient" pricing?  There
% was a time when aluminium was much more valuable than gold, as it
% were a new metal. 

Actually, if you wanted to be really interesting, you could have the
exchange rate vary over the course of the game (and by your charisma).
Then you could take away the $ status line, so the players would have
to feel their way around the prices.


